    "updated_at": "2021-12-30T09:50:14Z",
    "body": "@addyosmani, you will have a reason for filing this with the WHATWG (instead of with the CSS working group), but can you share more with those who can’t make the connection? Is it just to seek additional input, or to prepare a proposal for CSS that aligns with what we have in HTML?\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nThen, for both the HTML and a CSS solution, I wonder why our HTML solution and a prospective CSS-solution is so tightly coupled with “loading.” It’s just a loose thought, but what this seems to indicate is some sort of priority. The exciting thing about something like a priority is that it could offer more options for handling priority (and non-priority) resources other than loading sooner or later.\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n* `<img src=foo priority>` → UA to load asap; as it’s not tied to loading, a UA could also indicate importance on hover (light glow, whatever)\r\n* `img { background: bar; priority: low }` → UA to defer loading; as it’s not tied to loading alone, a UA could also suspend or delay animations on the respective element (or whatever)\r\n\r\nI didn’t follow the discussions around `loading` to tell whether something like this was considered or we missed an opportunity in HTML, so as to inform decisions for CSS.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
